\section*{Prime predecessors with even squarefree factor count}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 011; 2026-09-17), the asserted result is that infinitely many prime predecessors are squarefree with an even number of prime factors, equivalently $\mu(p-1)=1$.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
If $\Omega(n)$ counts prime factors with multiplicity, the asserted infinitude is
\[
 |\{p\text{ prime}:p-1\text{ squarefree},\ \Omega(p-1)\equiv0\pmod2\}|=\infty.
\]
Squarefreeness makes the same parity statement hold when prime factors are counted without multiplicity.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 011; 2026-09-17), the shifted-correlation framework is extended to a long factor that is itself prime and carries a common parity sign at both endpoints. Major-arc endpoint estimates, rough weights, and squarefree sieving then extract prime predecessors with the required sign.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The result supplies the conclusion in this problem with its displayed hypotheses and quantifiers. The broader statements and applications in the manuscript are kept separate from the particular question above. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 011; 2026-09-17).

\subsection{Formal verification}
